# Identity Resources & Access

## Resource types

| Type | Role | Duende ApiResource / scopes? |
|------|------|------------------------------|
| `Volume` | Annotatable image volume | Yes — scopes `{Name}.Read\|Annotate\|Review` |
| `SegmentationService` | Segmentation backend | Yes — scopes `{Name}.User\|Access Manager` |
| `Group` | User grouping / grants | No — management only |
| `OrganizationalUnit` | Hierarchy / ownership | No — management only (`Administrator`) |

API-facing types only: `Volume`, `SegmentationService`. Resource stores must filter to these when building ApiResources/ApiScopes.

## Names are unique per type, not globally

`IsResourceNameTaken` is scoped by `ResourceTypeId`. A **Group** and a **Volume** may share the same name (e.g. both `"Yiu"`).

When resolving permissions or token scopes **by name**:

- Prefer Volume / SegmentationService (`FindApiFacingResourceAsync`)
- Never use bare `Resource.FirstOrDefault(r => r.Name == name)` for volume login / scope checks — the Group row often wins by lower Id

```csharp
// BAD — may return Group "Yiu"
await context.Resource.FirstOrDefaultAsync(r => r.Name == name);

// GOOD
await context.FindApiFacingResourceAsync(name);
```

## Grants vs site admin

- Explicit access: `GrantedUserPermissions` + `GrantedGroupPermissions` (via recursive group membership, including virtual Anonymous)
- Site role `Administrator` (`Special.Roles.Admin`) has full access; list/token helpers short-circuit via `UserResourcePermissionsByType` / `IsUserPermitted`
- OrgUnit `Administrator` manages children in the **management UI**; it does **not** by itself populate volume OAuth scopes

## OAuth scopes

- Standard: `openid`, `profile`, `Viking.Annotation`
- Per volume: `{EncodedVolumeName}.Read`, `{EncodedVolumeName}.Annotate`, `{EncodedVolumeName}.Review`
- Spaces in `Resource.Name` are encoded as `-` in scopes and Permissions URL keys (`gRPC RC1 Test` → `gRPC-RC1-Test.Read`). Display names keep spaces.
- `FindApiFacingResourceAsync` accepts the display name or the encoded prefix
- `IsResourceNameTaken` treats encoded-prefix collisions as taken (`Foo Bar` vs `Foo-Bar`)
- Do not treat `Viking.Annotation` as a Volume named `Viking` when parsing `resource.permission` scopes
- Do not use `%20` inside OAuth scope tokens (form-urlencoded would decode it back to a space)

## Which process does what

| Process | Typical ports | Responsibility |
|---------|---------------|----------------|
| IdentityServerStandalone | `:5001` | Discovery, `/connect/token`, resource/client stores |
| WebManagement | `:4001` | MVC admin, MyAccess, `api/permissions/*` (cookie/bearer) |
| WebApi | `:6001` | `/Permissions/*` (introspection / anonymous username endpoints) |

MyAccess and management UIs should query `ApplicationDbContext` (or authenticated APIs) — never call WebApi `/Permissions/AccessibleVolumes` without a bearer token (returns `{}`).

VikingXML `VolumeToEndpoint IdentityApi` (or `Authentication`) must reach **WebApi** (`:6001`). A public host with no port (`https://identity.codepharm.net/Permissions/...`) hits IdentityServerStandalone or nginx→WebManagement and returns an unhandled 404 (`[IS]` / `[MGMT]`, “middleware pipeline without being handled”). Clients rewrite same-host IdentityApi URLs that are not on 6000/6001 to the WebApi port (`IdentityEndpoints.ResolvePermissionsApiUrl`).

## Client AllowedScopes

`IdentityServerVikingClientStore` must rebuild AllowedScopes from the live resource store. Do not permanently cache clients — new volumes would cause `invalid_scope` until restart.
